--TEST--
A double whose closure cannot stand in for the method it names is refused — `rule:testing/doubles`, `rule:types/callable-variance`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The third question the structural check asks, after the name is known to
// belong to the contract: `now` is declared and answered, and what answers it
// hands back a `string` where every call site dispatching through `Clock` was
// promised an `int`. A double that only matched names would pass this program
// and fail at the first call.
//
// The comparison is ordinary assignability over the answer's own signature, so
// `rule:types/callable-variance` is what a double is held to and nothing was
// invented for it: a closure may declare fewer parameters and accept wider
// ones, and only the unsound directions are refused.
interface Clock {
    public function now(): int;
}

Clock $clock = Core\Test::double<Clock>({
    now: fn(): string => "not a timestamp",
});
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0828]: `now:` answers `Clock::now` with `callable(): string`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
